   "gap": "Fisher 1922 sec. 8 joint Type III argument (pp.332-337): supply a multiparameter asymptotic-normality/efficiency theorem with explicit model, sampling and regularity scope (the owned body states at lines 338-341 and 403-412 that the multiparameter analogue is not proved), and with it either prove or explicitly bound the joint maximum-likelihood limit behind Fisher's shape efficiencies and his 100 per cent efficiency of m+a(p+1), stating the exact p-range and the parameter-dependent-support regularity it needs. | Fisher 1922 sec. 9 Pearson Type VII location/scaling efficiency of moments (pp.340-342) is not reconstructed: the location-scale theorem (owned body 427-452) treats only the Cauchy and Type III forms; supply the Type VII intrinsic-accuracy and moment-scaling efficiency calculation with its moment-existence regime (r>3, efficiency vanishing at r=3). | Fisher 1922 sec. 10 efficiency of the method of moments over the Pearsonian system (pp.342-355) is absent from the handbook route and owned files: reconstruct the Pearson-regime efficiency calculations with the moment and regularity conditions under which each stated efficiency is an information ratio. | Fisher 1922 sec. 11 near-normal efficiency of moments (pp.355-356) is absent: reconstruct the argument that moments are efficient in a small region about the normal curve, with the exact near-normal regime and the quartic-exponent family Fisher derives. | Fisher 1922 sec. 12 discontinuous distributions: the Poisson-series estimation argument (p.359) and the dilution-series information calculation (pp.363-366) are absent; reconstruct each with explicit sampling model, information calculation and regularity scope. | Grouped-data estimation (Fisher 1922 pp.356-363): the fixed-grid information periodic-remainder bound, existence/consistency/limit of the grouped maximum-likelihood estimate, and goodness-of-fit (chi-square) calibration are recorded as not established (fisher-grouping-and-moments.tex proofboundary lines 350-363; lines 214-219); supply them with stated premises or the exact correction where Fisher's small-bin statements overreach.",
